# ---------------------------------------------------------------- cost
# The instruction layer, which this module and c-13 were excluded from until
# now for one reason: every other c_cycles target measures per BYTE
# (unit_expr = "length($1)/2" over a hex field), and these corpora encode a
# STRUCTURE. A per-byte figure over "1,2,3" measures the decimal formatting of
# the corpus, not the walk.
#
# The unit here is the ELEMENT, read off the same field the harness parses:
# a comma-joined list, so the count is the number of commas plus one -- except
# for the empty field, which is zero and not one. gsub() returns how many
# substitutions it made, which is the comma count; substituting a comma for a
# comma is what makes it a counter rather than an edit.
#
# BUDGETS ARE MEASURED, never guessed, and only two of the three have one.
# A budget must be a number rather than a reference implementation -- it says
# where you stand without saying how to get there -- so the only honest source
# is a run against a correct implementation. ex02 and ex08 were measured at -O2
# over 3000 cases: a straightforward walk costs 4.8 instructions per element and
# a straightforward reverse 7.1, which is where the 5 and 8 below come from.
#
# ex14 gets NONE ON PURPOSE. Sorting is the one exercise here where the cost per
# element IS the algorithm, so any budget would quietly recommend the algorithm
# it was measured from -- which is the line between a target and an answer. The
# figure is reported without one.
#
# Nothing here can fail on these numbers: the layer never gates. A budget only
# changes the sentence printed underneath.
#
# syscall_budget = 0 IS assertable from the subject, and is the strongest thing
# a c_cycles target can say: none of these functions produces output, so any
# write() at all is a finding. That is a requirement the subject states by
# omission and the prototypes confirm -- they return void or a pointer, and
# nothing in them is asked to print.
# No spaces: sh_test tokenises `args`, and c_cycles fails at analysis if this
# contains any. awk does not need them.
ELEM = "($1==\"\"?0:gsub(/,/,\",\",$1)+1)"

c_cycles(
